1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,510
Quick question—how much
of your day disappears

2
00:00:02,530 --> 00:00:06,620
into answering the same
customer emails over and over.

3
00:00:06,640 --> 00:00:09,710
Where's my order? Can I change my address?

4
00:00:09,730 --> 00:00:11,810
How do I reset my password?

5
00:00:11,830 --> 00:00:16,190
Support matters, but the
repetitive parts quietly take over.

6
00:00:16,210 --> 00:00:19,160
The inbox does not
arrive neatly organized.

7
00:00:19,180 --> 00:00:22,190
You open a message, figure
out what type it is,

8
00:00:22,210 --> 00:00:25,070
search for the right
policy or past response,

9
00:00:25,090 --> 00:00:27,740
copy the link, and rewrite the explanation

10
00:00:27,760 --> 00:00:32,510
in a slightly different way.
Then you do that again and again.

11
00:00:32,530 --> 00:00:36,890
Meanwhile, tricky emails like
refunds, complaints, and chargebacks

12
00:00:36,910 --> 00:00:39,440
are mixed in with simple questions.

13
00:00:39,460 --> 00:00:41,570
That is where problems start.

14
00:00:41,590 --> 00:00:44,990
Response times slip. Tone
becomes inconsistent.

15
00:00:45,010 --> 00:00:46,940
Important details get missed,

16
00:00:46,960 --> 00:00:49,940
and customers receive
replies that feel cold.

17
00:00:49,960 --> 00:00:51,950
This is not a bad team issue.

18
00:00:51,970 --> 00:00:54,410
It is an inbox scale issue.

19
00:00:54,430 --> 00:00:56,360
Here is a cleaner approach.

20
00:00:56,380 --> 00:01:00,170
When a support email comes in,
the workflow classifies it:

21
00:01:00,190 --> 00:01:04,790
order status, account help,
billing, returns, complaint.

22
00:01:04,810 --> 00:01:07,670
Then pulls the relevant
information from a knowledge base

23
00:01:07,690 --> 00:01:11,480
and prepares a draft reply automatically.

24
00:01:11,500 --> 00:01:13,310
The draft matches the category

25
00:01:13,330 --> 00:01:15,920
and includes the correct steps and links.

26
00:01:15,940 --> 00:01:18,260
It is designed to be safe.

27
00:01:18,280 --> 00:01:21,290
Low-risk questions can
be drafted instantly,

28
00:01:21,310 --> 00:01:24,950
but high-risk topics, such as
refunds and angry complaints,

29
00:01:24,970 --> 00:01:27,260
are routed to human approval.

30
00:01:27,280 --> 00:01:29,630
The workflow does not guess.

31
00:01:29,650 --> 00:01:34,640
It drafts, flags, and waits for
a person to approve or edit.

32
00:01:34,660 --> 00:01:37,310
It also keeps responses consistent.

33
00:01:37,330 --> 00:01:39,890
Policies, tone, and
structure stay the same,

34
00:01:39,910 --> 00:01:42,110
so customers get clear answers

35
00:01:42,130 --> 00:01:45,200
and the team does not have
to start from scratch.

36
00:01:45,220 --> 00:01:47,960
If something is unclear, such
as a missing order number,

37
00:01:47,980 --> 00:01:50,330
it generates a short set
of follow-up questions

38
00:01:50,350 --> 00:01:52,430
instead of making assumptions.

39
00:01:52,450 --> 00:01:54,170
Everything is logged.

40
00:01:54,190 --> 00:01:58,010
The system records what came
in, how it was categorized,

41
00:01:58,030 --> 00:02:02,450
what draft was produced, who
approved it, and what was sent.

42
00:02:02,470 --> 00:02:05,360
If anything fails, such as missing data

43
00:02:05,380 --> 00:02:07,340
or an unavailable knowledge source,

44
00:02:07,360 --> 00:02:10,190
it fails safely and sends an alert.

45
00:02:10,210 --> 00:02:13,010
Here is an illustrative estimate.

46
00:02:13,030 --> 00:02:15,560
If an average support
email takes about 3 minutes

47
00:02:15,580 --> 00:02:18,350
to read, look up, and respond,

48
00:02:18,370 --> 00:02:22,640
and this reduces it to about 45
seconds of review for most messages,

49
00:02:22,660 --> 00:02:25,490
that saves roughly 2 minutes per email.

50
00:02:25,510 --> 00:02:27,410
At 500 emails a month,

51
00:02:27,430 --> 00:02:31,400
that is about 16 to 17
hours saved per month.

52
00:02:31,420 --> 00:02:33,080
This is based on assumptions

53
00:02:33,100 --> 00:02:35,300
and is not a guaranteed result.

54
00:02:35,320 --> 00:02:41,060
Independent portfolio prototype.
Fictional data. Results vary.

55
00:02:41,080 --> 00:02:44,690
The goal is faster replies,
more consistent support,

56
00:02:44,710 --> 00:02:46,850
and less time typing the same answers,

57
00:02:46,870 --> 00:02:51,080
so the team can focus on
customers who need a human.
